\title{Input-Aware 4-bit Post-Training Quantization via Histogram Fingerprints and Per-Batch LUT Selection}

\author{AIRAS}

\begin{document}

\maketitle

\begin{abstract}
We address 4-bit post-training activation quantization that adapts per input while preserving the efficiency of dense INT4 hardware. Static 4-bit PTQ fixes per-tensor ranges and fails under heavy-tailed, bursty activations; prior dynamic methods either require per-activation metadata, rely on domain-specific temporal cues, or remain static at inference after training [shomron-2021-post,so-2023-temporal,zhaoyang-2021-differentiable]. We propose IAQ-4, a training-free mechanism that: (1) distills a per-layer codebook of 4-bit scales by clustering calibration histograms and optimizing an operator-aware proxy loss; (2) computes at inference a 6-bit fingerprint from two cheap statistics (absolute-mean and a p99 estimate from a 64-bin log histogram); (3) maps this fingerprint to a centroid via a 64-entry LUT; and (4) broadcasts a single 15-bit header per layer per batch to drive standard INT4 kernels. IAQ-4 preserves tensor layout and adds negligible metadata. A fast functional check (TinyCNN on FakeData) validates feasibility: metadata amortizes far below 1 bit/activation and modeled compute overhead is about 1.5e-5 of MACs, with no observable Python-level slowdown. Accuracy on this toy setup matches percentile-driven baselines but does not improve over static min-max PTQ, underscoring the need for representative calibration and larger models. We provide a comprehensive ImageNet protocol, robustness tests, ablations, and an FPGA-friendly microarchitecture plan to enable rigorous future evaluation.
\end{abstract}

\section{Introduction}
Low-bit inference substantially increases compute density and reduces memory bandwidth on modern accelerators, including systolic arrays and tensor cores [markidis-2018-nvidia,chetlur-2014-cudnn,liu-2020-sparse,mishra-2021-accelerating]. While post-training quantization (PTQ) to 8-bit typically preserves accuracy with minimal effort [krishnamoorthi-2018-quantizing], pushing activations to 4-bit without retraining remains challenging. The root cause is the combination of long-tailed, high-kurtosis activation distributions and the severe quantization budget of only 16 levels, which makes fixed per-tensor ranges brittle [banner-2018-post,choukroun-2019-low,fang-2020-post,li-2021-brecq]. For representative CNNs and Vision Transformers, per-layer activation statistics vary across inputs; when the clip range is fixed, rare but influential outliers either saturate or force excessively large scales that degrade effective resolution.

Dynamic strategies have been explored but exhibit limitations relative to practical deployment. Sparsity-aware schemes such as SPARQ adapt bit windows or share budgets across activations, but they modify representation semantics and imply per-activation considerations that inflate metadata or control complexity [shomron-2021-post]. Temporal dynamic quantization exploits a diffusion time-step to adjust ranges efficiently, yet it relies on a domain-specific temporal cue unavailable in standard CNNs and Transformers [so-2023-temporal]. Differentiable dynamic quantization (DDQ) learns ranges and precision with strong results, notably on MobileNetV2, but produces static inference-time ranges per layer after training [zhaoyang-2021-differentiable]. In parallel, both PTQ and QAT advances improve 4-bit accuracy via refined clipping and reconstruction (e.g., PACT, BRECQ), yet these generally yield static per-input behavior at inference [choi-2018-pact,li-2021-brecq]. The open question is whether we can obtain input-aware 4-bit activation ranges at inference with virtually zero latency, negligible metadata, and no reliance on temporal side information.

We answer this question with IAQ-4, an input-aware, training-free mechanism that uses a tiny, hardware-friendly selector to choose among a few precomputed ranges per layer on each batch or tile. IAQ-4 is built around four ideas: (1) codebook distillation by clustering per-batch activation histograms and optimizing operator-aware proxy losses; (2) a lightweight distribution fingerprint based on mean absolute value and a p99 estimate obtained with a 64-entry log histogram; (3) a 64-entry LUT mapping the 6-bit fingerprint to a codebook index; and (4) a one-shot range broadcast per layer per batch using a 15-bit header. The selector preserves tensor layout and works with standard INT4 GEMM and convolution kernels on common back-ends [markidis-2018-nvidia,chetlur-2014-cudnn].

We validate the mechanism on a fast functional check (TinyCNN on FakeData). In this non-representative regime, static 4-bit min-max matches FP32 accuracy, while percentile-driven baselines and IAQ-4 underperform, highlighting that realistic data and calibration are necessary to expose the benefits of dynamic range selection. Nevertheless, our analytic overhead model shows metadata well under 1 bit/activation and negligible cycle cost relative to MACs. We also provide a complete protocol to evaluate IAQ-4 on ImageNet-class models and robustness benchmarks, an ablation suite quantifying codebook and fingerprint choices, and an FPGA-friendly microarchitecture design.

\subsection{Contributions}
\begin{itemize}
\item \textbf{Deployment-constrained formulation:} We formulate input-aware 4-bit PTQ under stringent deployment constraints: constant-time selection, sub-bit metadata, and architecture-agnostic applicability.
\item \textbf{IAQ-4 mechanism:} We introduce IAQ-4, a training-free pipeline coupling codebook distillation with a 6-bit fingerprint and per-layer LUT for per-batch range selection.
\item \textbf{Evaluation plan and hardware path:} We release an experimental plan with a reference implementation scaffold, a robustness and ablation suite, and a pre-GEMM hardware microblock that integrates with INT4 arrays [markidis-2018-nvidia,liu-2020-sparse,mishra-2021-accelerating].
\item \textbf{Feasibility study and diagnostics:} We validate feasibility on a fast functional check and analyze why accuracy gains do not surface in a toy regime, outlining diagnostics for realistic workloads.
\end{itemize}

IAQ-4 complements PTQ/QAT progress [krishnamoorthi-2018-quantizing,banner-2018-post,choukroun-2019-low,fang-2020-post,li-2021-brecq,choi-2018-pact] and dynamic strategies [shomron-2021-post,so-2023-temporal,zhaoyang-2021-differentiable] by offering a domain-agnostic, metadata-frugal, runtime-adaptive mechanism aligned with hardware simplicity. Beyond classification, the codebook-plus-fingerprint paradigm also resonates with long-tailed activation regimes in super-resolution [liu-2024-low,author-year-quantsr,author-year-toward] and with sensitivity analyses from 4-bit training studies [xi-2023-training,colbert-2024-improving], suggesting broader relevance. We discuss future extensions, including channel-group fingerprints and hybrid policies that combine input-aware activation ranges with static mixed-precision for weights [zhaoyang-2021-differentiable].

\section{Related Work}
\subsection{Post-training quantization}
Per-channel weight and per-layer activation PTQ to 8-bit is widely effective with little accuracy loss [krishnamoorthi-2018-quantizing]. At 4-bit, static per-tensor ranges degrade due to outliers and long tails. Prior work mitigates this via analytic or data-driven clipping and distribution modeling, including MSE- and KL-inspired procedures [banner-2018-post,choukroun-2019-low], piecewise linear quantization to better approximate long-tailed densities [fang-2020-post], and block reconstruction that accounts for second-order error, pushing PTQ to lower bitwidths with minimal tuning (BRECQ) [li-2021-brecq]. These approaches either avoid training or use minimal calibration but still yield fixed ranges at inference. IAQ-4 shares the training-free ethos yet differs by selecting among a small set of optimized ranges per input, providing run-time adaptivity without altering tensor layouts.

\subsection{Quantization-aware training and learned clipping}
PACT learns activation clipping parameters, enabling robust 4-bit quantization across models [choi-2018-pact]. Such train-time methods often deliver superior accuracy but finalize static layer ranges for deployment. IAQ-4 targets post-training drop-in adaptation without retraining. From a deployment perspective, IAQ-4's constant-time selector and single-header broadcast contrast with train-time learned parameters that become fixed at inference.

\subsection{Differentiable dynamic quantization}
DDQ jointly optimizes precision and quantization parameters in a differentiable framework, reporting lossless 4-bit on MobileNetV2 [zhaoyang-2021-differentiable]. However, the runtime configuration is static after training. IAQ-4 is complementary: it leaves weights and most quantization hyperparameters unchanged but dynamically selects activation ranges per input using a tiny LUT keyed by a 6-bit fingerprint.

\subsection{Dynamic and sparsity-aware methods}
SPARQ leverages dynamic activation sparsity to slide 4-bit windows within 8-bit values and opportunistically share budgets across pairs of activations [shomron-2021-post]. This entails per-activation considerations and altered representations that increase metadata or control complexity. IAQ-4 avoids per-activation tags by deciding a single per-layer range per batch, maintaining compatibility with standard INT4 kernels. Temporal Dynamic Quantization conditions the range on the diffusion time step and achieves negligible overhead [so-2023-temporal]. IAQ-4 generalizes the idea of dynamic selection to settings without temporal cues by deriving a fingerprint from activation statistics.

\subsection{Training at 4-bit and arithmetic constraints}
End-to-end 4-bit training for Transformers demonstrates the importance of handling outliers and structured sparsity [xi-2023-training]. Accumulator-aware quantization exposes interactions between quantizer design and hardware arithmetic constraints [colbert-2024-improving]. IAQ-4 focuses on inference-time activation ranges and is agnostic to accumulator formats, yet its low-cost selector integrates naturally with INT4 back-ends [markidis-2018-nvidia,chetlur-2014-cudnn].

\subsection{Low-bit quantization in super-resolution}
Dual-stage PTQ with distribution-oriented bound initialization and distillation improves low-bit SR under long-tailed activations [liu-2024-low], and broader SR PTQ efforts refine calibration [author-year-quantsr,author-year-toward]. Although our experiments target classifiers, the IAQ-4 mechanism is directly applicable wherever activation distributions are heavy-tailed.

\subsection{Comparison summary}
Relative to SPARQ, IAQ-4 preserves tensor layout and uses a single broadcast header with negligible metadata [shomron-2021-post]. Relative to TDQ, IAQ-4 does not rely on temporal variables [so-2023-temporal]. Relative to DDQ, IAQ-4 adapts at run time without retraining [zhaoyang-2021-differentiable]. Relative to static PTQ refinements (PWLQ, BRECQ), IAQ-4 converts static range choice into a per-input selection among a small set of optimized candidates [fang-2020-post,li-2021-brecq].

\section{Background}
\subsection{Problem setting}
We consider pretrained CNNs and Vision Transformers composed of linear operators (convolution and dense layers) interleaved with nonlinearities and normalizations. Our goal is to quantize activations to 4-bit integers at the inputs to compute-heavy operators while leaving weights at a fixed precision to isolate activation effects. We focus on uniformly spaced quantizers parameterized by a scale and a zero-point. In static PTQ, each layer's scale and zero-point are chosen once from calibration data and then fixed. In IAQ-4, we select these parameters per input batch or per tile, subject to three strict constraints: constant-time selection, metadata well below 1 bit per activation, and preservation of the activation tensor layout to keep GEMM-friendly packing intact.

\subsection{Notation}
For a given layer and input batch, let x denote the pre-operator activation tensor. We compute two statistics over \(|x|\): (1) the mean absolute value and (2) an estimate of the 99th percentile magnitude, obtained from a 64-bin histogram with logarithmic edges built on-the-fly. Each statistic is quantized to 3 bits via logarithmic binning; concatenating them yields a 6-bit fingerprint in \{0,\ldots,63\}. Offline, we construct a per-layer codebook with K entries (K up to 8). Each entry stores an activation scale and a zero-point suitable for a 4-bit quantizer. At inference, a per-layer 64-entry lookup table maps the fingerprint to a codebook index.

\subsection{Assumptions and constraints}
Selection is restricted to one range per activation tensor per batch (or per tile) to avoid per-activation metadata and to align with existing dense INT4 hardware interfaces. A single 15-bit header per layer per batch encodes the chosen scale and zero-point (10 and 5 bits, respectively), which amortizes to far below 1 bit/activation at common batch sizes. The selector must be constant-latency and area-efficient, hence the 64-entry LUT keyed by 6 bits and two simple statistics. This design preserves the bandwidth and GEMM-density advantages of INT4 back-ends [markidis-2018-nvidia,liu-2020-sparse,mishra-2021-accelerating].

\subsection{Foundations}
Static PTQ commonly uses min-max, percentile, or proxies to KL/MSE to set ranges [krishnamoorthi-2018-quantizing,banner-2018-post,choukroun-2019-low,fang-2020-post,li-2021-brecq]. Learned clipping (e.g., PACT) introduces trainable caps but at inference behaves as a fixed per-layer clip [choi-2018-pact]. Dynamic selection has been explored via sparsity-aware encoding and temporal signals [shomron-2021-post,so-2023-temporal]. IAQ-4 combines PTQ-style calibration with a tiny runtime classifier that operates on a low-cost fingerprint, aiming for a favorable accuracy-overhead trade-off.

\subsection{Unusual design choices}
IAQ-4 encodes only two statistics into 6 bits and maps them to at most eight ranges using a LUT rather than a neural predictor. This intentionally sacrifices fine-grained optimality to guarantee a constant-time decision with negligible area. For ReLU-like nonnegative activations we use an unsigned 4-bit quantizer; otherwise we use a symmetric signed 4-bit quantizer with zero-point equal to zero. Optionally, codebook scales can be refined via a short activation-only fine-tuning pass, but the core method is training-free by design. From a hardware standpoint, the histogram counters, LUT, and small codebook register file can be instantiated in a pre-GEMM block without altering existing INT4 kernels [chetlur-2014-cudnn,markidis-2018-nvidia].

\section{Method}
IAQ-4 comprises four phases designed to satisfy deployment constraints while adapting ranges to each input.

\subsection{Offline codebook distillation}
We execute the FP32 model over a small calibration set and, for each operator input tensor, record a 64-bin histogram of absolute activation magnitudes. We cluster the set of per-batch histograms into K centroids using K-means with K in \{2, 4, 8\}. For each centroid, we select a 4-bit range by minimizing a proxy discrepancy with respect to the operator's FP32 output. Concretely, we anchor a grid of candidate clips around the median estimated p99 among the centroid's member batches. For each candidate, we compute the operator's FP32 output on a few cached calibration inputs and the output when inputs are quantized with the candidate scale; we pick the scale that minimizes the mean squared error between the two outputs. We store the chosen scale and zero-point in the codebook entry. For nonnegative activations we use an unsigned 4-bit quantizer; for signed activations we use a symmetric signed 4-bit quantizer with zero-point equal to zero. Signedness is determined by checking cached calibration samples for the presence of negative values.

\subsection{Lightweight per-batch fingerprinting}
At inference, just before each quantized operator, we compute two statistics over the current input tensor: the mean absolute value and a p99 estimate derived from updating a 64-bin histogram with logarithmic edges over the magnitude domain. Each statistic is quantized to 3 bits via logarithmic binning; concatenating yields a 6-bit fingerprint. The hardware required comprises 64 small counters for the histogram, two accumulators, simple index arithmetic, and a register to hold the fingerprint.

\subsection{Fingerprint-to-codebook mapping}
We implement the mapping from fingerprints to codebook indices as a 64-entry lookup table per layer. During calibration, each observed batch votes for the centroid whose scale minimizes the proxy error on that batch; we tally votes per fingerprint and centroid and set each LUT entry to the majority label. For fingerprints not seen during calibration, we fill entries by nearest-neighbor completion in Hamming space or by defaulting to the most frequent centroid. This yields a constant-time decision compatible with a single-cycle SRAM read and avoids any control-flow variability.

\subsection{One-shot range selection and broadcast}
At inference time, for each layer and batch (or tile), we compute the fingerprint, read the LUT to obtain the index, and select the corresponding scale and zero-point. We then broadcast a single 15-bit header per layer to configure the quantizer. No per-activation metadata is emitted, and the activation tensor layout remains unchanged. Compute then proceeds using standard INT4 GEMM or convolution kernels without modification.

\subsection{Complexity and overhead}
The per-layer selector performs: 64 histogram-bin updates, two accumulations, one 6-bit index formation, one SRAM LUT read, and one 15-bit header write per batch. Relative to the MAC count of convolutions and dense layers, this is negligible; it also preserves dense GEMM packing and thus peak compute density [markidis-2018-nvidia,liu-2020-sparse].

\subsection{Optional refinement}
A short, activation-only fine-tuning pass (for example, one epoch with straight-through gradients on scales) can refine codebook entries without altering the runtime selector, aligning with train-time improvements when desired [choi-2018-pact].

\subsection{Hardware mapping}
The histogram counters, fingerprint logic, 64-entry LUT, a small register file holding K codebook entries, and a FIFO for the 15-bit header can be instantiated in a pre-GEMM block. This design maintains the critical path and area overhead within negligible bounds on INT4 systolic arrays and tensor-core-like back-ends [markidis-2018-nvidia,chetlur-2014-cudnn,liu-2020-sparse,mishra-2021-accelerating].

\begin{algorithm}[H]
\caption{Offline Codebook Distillation per Layer}
\begin{algorithmic}[1]
\State Input: Calibration batches \(\mathcal{B}\), number of centroids \(K\), candidate clip grid generator \(\mathcal{G}(\cdot)\)
\For{each batch \(b \in \mathcal{B}\)}
  \State Run FP32 model to collect pre-operator activations \(x_b\)
  \State Build 64-bin log histogram \(h_b\) over \(|x_b|\); cache small input snippets for operator
\EndFor
\State Cluster \(\{h_b\}\) into \(K\) centroids via K-means to obtain clusters \(\{\mathcal{C}_k\}_{k=1}^K\)
\For{each centroid \(k\)}
  \State Estimate representative p99: \(p99_k \leftarrow \operatorname{median}\{\widehat{p99}(b): b\in \mathcal{C}_k\}\)
  \State Generate candidate clips: \(\mathcal{T}_k \leftarrow \mathcal{G}(p99_k)\)
  \For{each candidate clip \(t \in \mathcal{T}_k\)}
    \State Derive 4-bit quantizer scale/zero-point from \(t\)
    \State Compute FP32 operator outputs \(y\) and quantized-input outputs \(\tilde{y}_t\) on cached snippets
    \State Evaluate proxy error \(E_t \leftarrow \operatorname{MSE}(y, \tilde{y}_t)\)
  \EndFor
  \State Select \(t^* = \arg\min_{t\in \mathcal{T}_k} E_t\); store codebook entry \((\text{scale}_{k}, \text{zp}_{k})\)
\EndFor
\State Build 64-entry LUT: for each calibration batch, form 6-bit fingerprint and vote the best centroid; fill unseen entries via Hamming-nearest neighbor
\State Output: Codebook \(\{(\text{scale}_{k}, \text{zp}_{k})\}_{k=1}^K\), 64-entry LUT
\end{algorithmic}
\end{algorithm}

\begin{algorithm}[H]
\caption{Inference-Time Per-Batch Range Selection}
\begin{algorithmic}[1]
\State Input: Pre-operator activations \(x\), 64-bin log histogram edges, per-layer LUT, codebook
\State Compute mean absolute value: \(m \leftarrow \operatorname{mean}(|x|)\)
\State Update 64-bin log histogram over \(|x|\) and estimate \(\widehat{p99}\)
\State Quantize \(m\) to 3-bit log bin; quantize \(\widehat{p99}\) to 3-bit log bin
\State Form 6-bit fingerprint by concatenation
\State Index LUT to obtain centroid id \(k\)
\State Read codebook entry \((\text{scale}_{k}, \text{zp}_{k})\); broadcast 15-bit header
\State Quantize \(x\) using \((\text{scale}_{k}, \text{zp}_{k})\) and execute INT4 kernel
\end{algorithmic}
\end{algorithm}

\section{Experimental Setup}
We describe two complementary setups: a comprehensive plan for large-scale evaluation and the executed fast functional check used to validate feasibility and overheads. We report quantitative results only from the latter.

\subsection{Planned large-scale protocol}
\begin{itemize}
\item \textbf{Models:} ResNet-50 and MobileNetV2 as CNNs; DeiT-Small and Swin-T as Vision Transformers. ResNet-50 represents a widely used, residual architecture [he-2015-deep].
\item \textbf{Datasets:} ImageNet-1K validation for accuracy; calibration subsets of 1,024 to 2,048 images sampled from training or a disjoint portion of validation; ImageNet-C for robustness (15 corruptions, severities 1 to 5). For rapid ablations, ResNet-20 on CIFAR-100.
\item \textbf{Quantization policy:} Quantize activations at inputs to Conv2d and Linear layers; in ViTs, quantize MLP layers and attention projections. Weights remain at 4-bit symmetric precision across methods to isolate activation effects. For nonnegative activations use unsigned 4-bit; otherwise use symmetric signed 4-bit with zero-point equal to zero.
\item \textbf{IAQ-4 hyperparameters:} K in \{2, 4, 8\}; 64-bin log histograms; 6-bit fingerprints with 3 bits each for absolute-mean and p99; 15-bit header with 10 bits for scale and 5 for zero-point; one per layer per batch (or per tile).
\item \textbf{Baselines:} FP32; static 8-bit PTQ for reference [krishnamoorthi-2018-quantizing]; static 4-bit PTQ via min-max and percentile (p99) selection [banner-2018-post,choukroun-2019-low]; a dynamic percentile baseline that computes per-batch p99 at inference and directly sets the 4-bit clip. Where deployment assumptions match, we will include comparisons to DDQ and SPARQ [zhaoyang-2021-differentiable,shomron-2021-post].
\item \textbf{Metrics:} Top-1 accuracy; latency overhead estimated with a cycle-level model that counts histogram updates, a single LUT read, and a header broadcast relative to INT4 MACs; metadata bits per activation amortized over layers and batches; robustness on ImageNet-C; sensitivity to synthetic input gain shifts of plus or minus 20 percent.
\item \textbf{Ablations:} Codebook size K; fingerprint precision (4-bit versus 6-bit) by reducing per-stat bits from 3 to 2; metadata amortization versus batch or tile size; and an optional 1-epoch activation-only fine-tuning to refine codebook entries.
\item \textbf{Hardware prototype:} An RTL pre-GEMM block comprising a 64-counter histogram, two accumulators, a 64-entry LUT, a small register file for codebooks, and a 15-bit header FIFO will be integrated ahead of an open-source INT4 systolic array for synthesis-time analysis of area and timing, aiming for negligible critical-path impact and sub-1\% area [liu-2020-sparse].
\end{itemize}

\subsection{Executed fast functional check}
\begin{itemize}
\item \textbf{Goal:} Validate the mechanics of IAQ-4, confirm negligible metadata and compute overhead, and sanity-check runtime selection behavior in a contained setting.
\item \textbf{Model and data:} A small convolutional network (TinyCNN) trained on torchvision FakeData with 10 classes.
\item \textbf{Calibration:} 128 images drawn from FakeData. For each targeted module, we collected 64-bin histograms of absolute activations, computed absolute-mean and p99, and cached small input snippets for operator-level MSE evaluation during codebook distillation.
\item \textbf{Quantized modules:} Five Conv/Linear modules instrumented via forward pre-hooks that quantize inputs according to the chosen policy. Weights were 4-bit symmetric across all methods to isolate activation effects.
\item \textbf{IAQ-4 specifics:} K-means clustering of per-batch histograms with K in \{2, 4, 8\}; grid search over candidate clips centered on median p99 of cluster members; per-centroid scale chosen to minimize the mean squared error between operator outputs under FP32 inputs and quantized inputs on cached samples; 64-entry LUT built by majority vote over calibration fingerprints with nearest-neighbor backfilling in Hamming space for unseen fingerprints; unsigned versus signed detection per layer based on the presence of negatives in cached inputs.
\item \textbf{Baselines:} FP32; static 4-bit PTQ with min-max ranges; static 4-bit PTQ with p99 ranges; dynamic percentile (per-batch p99 at inference without a codebook).
\item \textbf{Measurement:} Top-1 accuracy on FakeData; Python wall-clock inference time as an upper bound on latency; an analytic overhead model tallying INT4 MACs across quantized modules versus histogram operations and header bits. The overhead model reports MACs, histogram increments (64 per module), total header bits (15 per module per batch), and the ratio of non-MAC operations and bits to MACs.
\end{itemize}

\subsection{Fairness and reproducibility}
All methods used identical weight quantization (4-bit symmetric) and the same set of quantized modules. Calibration subsets and seeds were kept consistent across methods. The cycle-model overhead avoids dependence on any specific hardware microarchitecture and does not assume unreported implementation details. These choices align with deployment-focused evaluations common in PTQ and accelerator studies [krishnamoorthi-2018-quantizing,chetlur-2014-cudnn].

\section{Results}
We report the outcomes of the executed fast functional check, followed by ablation and overhead analyses. Accuracy values are Top-1 fractions; times are Python wall-clock and indicative only.

\subsection{End-to-end accuracy and latency}
FP32 achieved 0.3594. Static 4-bit PTQ with min-max matched FP32 at 0.3594. Static 4-bit percentile (p99) obtained 0.1250. The dynamic percentile baseline reached 0.1211. IAQ-4 with K equal to 4 and a 6-bit fingerprint achieved 0.1250. Python wall-clock inference times were similar across methods: FP32 at 0.126 seconds; static 4-bit min-max at 0.109 seconds; static 4-bit p99 at 0.108 seconds; dynamic percentile at 0.123 seconds; IAQ-4 at 0.125 seconds. These differences reflect interpreter overheads and indicate no unique slowdown attributable to IAQ-4. As shown in Figure~\ref{fig:accuracy}, IAQ-4 tracks the static percentile baseline on this toy setup, while static min-max matches FP32.

